2013-11-19 16:43 — Janis Papanagnou:
> Am 19.11.2013 14:32, schrieb Jonadab the Unsightly One:
>> Candles appear on the floor of the Mines in a lot of
>> variants, including Slash'em.  The rationale was, it's
>> easier to code up than guaranteeing that Izchak will
>> always have at least seven of them to sell you, and
>> nobody wants to get to the end of Gehennom and have
>> to polypile just for candles.
> In the few games where not enough candles had been
> generated at the lighting store, I either found
> random ones lying around, got some from a polypile,
> found some in bones, or wished for them, if nothing
> else helped. I don't find anything wrong with a game
> that doesn't guarantee everything. Rather I find the
> variance to think about solutions and make decisions
> an interesting and even essential part of that game
> of Nethack.
It does guarantee everything — in a way.  You'll get a guaranteed wand
of wishing at the Castle, engraving with said wand will certainly grant
you a wish, and in a single wish up to seven candles are guaranteed (as
long as you didn't do something seriously wrong shortly before).
There's a 100% sure way of dealing with an avaricious Izchak.  Of
course, you might have to make some compromises elsewhere if the RNG is
particularly nasty.

But I do like the solution of handing more monsters a candle.  After
all, some kind of candle (or torch) does seem like a proper tool for
exploring a dungeon.  I'd rather like to see them used in this way too.
 ;-)  Maybe consider slightly reducing generation of (at least
non-magic) lamps for balance.

